+# Blocked: `RateLimiter.acquire_async()` does not exist in vendored ratelimit
+
+## What was requested
+
+Migrate `Client` to the platform's new native async limiter API:
+
+- add `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)` that awaits `limiter.acquire_async()` before
+  each request,
+- remove the `_ThreadedLimiter` (`run_in_executor`) workaround,
+- without editing `vendor/`, which is synced from the platform repo.
+
+## Why it is blocked
+
+The checked-in vendor copy is **ratelimit 2.4.1**, and it has no async API.
+
+Evidence in `vendor/ratelimit.py`:
+
+- `__version__ = "2.4.1"`
+- `RateLimiter` defines only `try_acquire()` and the blocking `acquire()`.
+- No `acquire_async` method (or any `async`/`await` code) is present.
+- Internals are `threading.Lock` + `time.sleep`; there is nothing that can be
+  awaited natively.
+
+`vendor/CHANGELOG.md` likewise stops at 2.4.1 (latest entry: refill drift fix)
+and does not mention async support in any released version.
+
+The supplied tests confirm this:
+
+```
+$ python3 -m unittest -v
+ERROR: test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter
+  AttributeError: 'RateLimiter' object has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+ERROR: test_limiter_is_natively_async
+  AttributeError: type object 'RateLimiter' has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+Ran 3 tests ... FAILED (errors=2)
+```
+
+Because `vendor/` may not be hand-edited, and because the instruction is not to
+work around a missing API, there is no correct implementation of
+`fetch_all_async` using a native limiter:
+
+- Calling `self.limiter.acquire_async()` would raise `AttributeError` at
+  runtime, so shipping that method would be broken code.
+- Monkeypatching/adding an `acquire_async` shim in `client.py` (e.g. wrapping
+  `acquire` in `run_in_executor`) would fake the platform API and would also
+  fail `test_limiter_is_natively_async`, which inspects the vendored
+  `RateLimiter` class itself.
+- Editing `vendor/ratelimit.py` is explicitly out of bounds.
+
+## Current state
+
+- `client.py` is unchanged. `fetch_all()` works and its test passes.
+- `_ThreadedLimiter` is retained: it is currently unused by `fetch_all`, but it
+  is the only async-capable path against 2.4.1 and removing it now would leave
+  no async support at all. It can be deleted as part of the real migration.
+- `vendor/` is untouched.
+
+## What unblocks this
+
+Re-sync `vendor/` from platform-libs at a release that actually exports
+`RateLimiter.acquire_async()` (verify with
+`python3 -c "import asyncio, inspect; from vendor.ratelimit import RateLimiter; print(inspect.iscoroutinefunction(RateLimiter.acquire_async))"`).
+Then:
+
+1. Add `fetch_all_async` awaiting `self.limiter.acquire_async()` before each
+   `await self.fetch(url)`.
+2. Delete `_ThreadedLimiter` and the now-unused `asyncio` import if nothing else
+   needs it.
+3. Run `python3 -m unittest -v`; all three tests should pass.
